<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  lang="en"
  dir="ltr"
  hreflang={hreflang}
  schema={schema}
  lastmod="2026-10-09"
>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="container hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__content">
        <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} lang="en" />
        <h1 class="hero__h1">Online Divorce in the UAE: What You Can Do Online, Court by Court</h1>
        <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated October 2026</p>
        <p class="hero__sub">
          Most steps of a UAE divorce can now be done online: filing, paying, being served, attending
          by video and receiving the judgment. What you cannot do is get divorced by a website. The
          divorce still comes from a court, and a few steps still need you, or someone holding your
          power of attorney, in person.
        </p>
        <ul class="hero__trust">
          <li>✓ Civil Procedure Code rules by article</li>
          <li>✓ Dubai, Abu Dhabi, federal courts &amp; RAK</li>
          <li>✓ Fees from official court service pages</li>
          <li>✓ What no website can do for you</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__form">
        <LeadForm lang="en" variant="hero" heading="Filing From Home or Abroad? Ask First" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="container main-content">
    <figure style="margin:0 0 8px;">
      <img src="/images/online-divorce-uae.webp" alt="Online divorce in the UAE: a laptop, phone and leather folder on a walnut desk by a window overlooking Dubai at night" width="1200" height="675" loading="eager" decoding="async" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px;" />
    </figure>

    <!-- Short answer -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="answer-heading">
      <h2 id="answer-heading">What &ldquo;Online Divorce&rdquo; Means in the UAE</h2>
      <p>
        In the UAE, &ldquo;online divorce&rdquo; means an ordinary court divorce handled through the
        court&rsquo;s digital channels, not a separate, faster kind of divorce. Federal Decree-Law No. 42
        of 2022, the Civil Procedure Code, lets a case be filed electronically, served by text message or
        email, heard by video and decided in an electronic judgment. Each court runs its own portal, and
        each still sets limits.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>The law is the same online or on paper.</strong> Muslim couples fall under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024; non-Muslims under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022, or in Abu Dhabi under Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 at the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court.</li>
        <li><strong>The court is the same too.</strong> Dubai, Abu Dhabi and Ras Al Khaimah run their own courts; Sharjah, Ajman, Fujairah and Umm Al Quwain use the federal courts.</li>
        <li><strong>Some steps stay physical.</strong> Signing a power of attorney abroad, documenting a talaq in Dubai and, in Abu Dhabi, lodging the civil divorce form at a typing centre.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">3</span>
          <span class="stat-label">business days to pay the fee after the payment notice</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">10</span>
          <span class="stat-label">business days for the other spouse to file a defence</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">AED 100</span>
          <span class="stat-label">per Dubai personal status application, capped at AED 2,000</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">AED 5,000</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Abu Dhabi civil divorce application</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-box info-box--blue" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <h3>How this site earns money</h3>
        <p>
          DivorceU.AE is a referral service, not a law firm. It earns when readers ask to be put in touch
          with a UAE-licensed family lawyer. Everything below can be done without one; the page says where
          help tends to pay for itself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Civil Procedure Code -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="rules-heading">
      <h2 id="rules-heading">What the Civil Procedure Code Allows You to Do Online</h2>
      <p>
        The Civil Procedure Code allows almost every stage of a case to happen electronically: Article
        330 says fee collection, registration, service, documents, attendance, pleading, witnesses,
        judgments, appeals and execution are all valid if done wholly or partly remotely. The code
        applies in every emirate, so these rules hold whichever court hears your divorce.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Stage</th><th>What the law says</th><th>Article</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Filing the claim</td><td>A case starts with a statement of claim filed with the Case Management Office in electronic or paper format, under the court&rsquo;s own procedures.</td><td>44(1)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Paying the fee</td><td>The case is registered once fees are collected. Pay within 3 business days from the day after the payment notice, or the registration is void.</td><td>47(1)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Service on your spouse</td><td>By recorded audio or video call, text message, smart applications, email, fax or other modern means; also by hand at home, work or an elected domicile; failing that, by posting on the court&rsquo;s website.</td><td>9(1)(a)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>When service counts</td><td>From the date the email, text, fax or recorded message is sent; for a website posting, from the date of posting.</td><td>11</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The defence</td><td>Filed electronically or on paper within 10 business days of service. Where the file is electronic, the court lets the parties review the claim on its system or sends it to them.</td><td>48(2), 49</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hearings</td><td>The court president, circuit president or judge may take any procedure remotely, at any stage.</td><td>331</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Asking for an in-person hearing</td><td>In a remote trial, any party may ask at each hearing for the trial to be held in person. The court decides after notifying the other parties.</td><td>333</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Records</td><td>Remote hearings are recorded, kept electronically and confidential.</td><td>334</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The judgment</td><td>An enforceable copy obtained remotely carries the writ of execution and an electronic signature. Certified copies can be delivered electronically or on paper.</td><td>131(1), 131(3)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>E-signatures</td><td>Electronic signatures and documents have the same authenticity as official paper ones if they meet the Electronic Transactions and Trust Services law.</td><td>338</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022, official English translation. The Arabic text prevails.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        Two of these rules are easy to miss. The three-day fee window is short, and a missed payment
        voids the filing, so pay the same day the notice arrives. And because electronic service counts
        from the moment of sending, a text from the court that you overlook does not stop the clock on
        your 10-day defence period. A court notice sent through a messaging app is also a different thing
        from a spouse&rsquo;s message announcing a divorce; for that, see
        <a href="/en/whatsapp-divorce-uae/">whether a WhatsApp divorce is valid in the UAE</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Article 328 defines remote communication technology as audiovisual communication for remote
        attendance and the exchange of documents, covering registration, service, trial and execution. A
        remote hearing is a tool the judge may use, not a right. What you can do is ask for the opposite:
        if you would rather be in the room, for example with an interpreter beside you, Article 333 lets
        you ask at each hearing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Court by court -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="courts-heading">
      <h2 id="courts-heading">Court by Court: Where Your Divorce Is Handled Online</h2>
      <p>
        Which portal you use depends on which court hears the case. The UAE Government portal confirms
        that Abu Dhabi, Dubai and Ras Al Khaimah keep their own judicial departments, while Sharjah,
        Ajman, Fujairah and Umm Al Quwain follow the federal judicial system run under the Ministry of
        Justice. Here is what each one offers for family cases, based on the courts&rsquo; own service
        pages.
      </p>

      <h3>Dubai Courts</h3>
      <p>
        Dubai Courts takes family cases online through its website and through the government service
        centres known as Al Adheed, and it also runs a smart app. You need a registered account;
        individuals sign in with UAE PASS. Two services matter for a divorce.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>Family Conciliation</h3>
          <p>
            The Family Guidance file. Open to a citizen or resident of Dubai, or someone working there.
            You upload your marriage contract as a PDF. There is no fee. The journey is: register the
            file, attend the sessions, receive a decision, which can be a settlement agreement, a waiver,
            closure of the file or a referral to the competent court.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Personal Status Claims</h3>
          <p>
            The divorce claim itself. You upload the statement of claim and a docket of supporting
            documents, plus the referral from the Family Conciliation section. One party must be a citizen
            or resident of Dubai. Fee: AED 100 per application, capped at AED 2,000. The result is an
            electronic first-instance judgment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The claims page still lists the Family Conciliation referral among the uploads. Non-Muslim civil
        divorces are not referred to Family Guidance (Article 3 of the 2022 law), so in a civil case,
        check with the court or a typing office what the system asks for at that step. One step is not
        online at all. Dubai Courts&rsquo; service for certifying uncontested personal status
        declarations (ishhadat) requires you, or an agent holding a special official power of attorney,
        to appear with your original ID before a judge or certifier, with two witnesses, for a fee of
        AED 220. The full Dubai sequence, including timelines, is in our guide on
        <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-dubai/">how to file for divorce in Dubai</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Abu Dhabi Judicial Department</h3>
      <p>
        Abu Dhabi handles the two tracks differently, and neither is online from start to finish.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Muslim couples, Family Guidance.</strong> The ADJD registers the family dispute through its electronic services, reached through UAE PASS. Its Family Guidance page says sessions are held remotely by videoconference: you install Webex, and both spouses receive an email with the session link on the day. If there is no settlement, the request goes to the competent court at the request of either spouse.</li>
        <li><strong>Non-Muslims, Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court.</strong> The court publishes a No-Fault Divorce Application form and a Defendant&rsquo;s Response form, but its page tells applicants to submit the application at an Abu Dhabi typing centre. Documents: a copy of the marriage certificate in English and Arabic, and copies of passport and Emirates ID. The fee is AED 5,000.</li>
        <li><strong>After filing, it is digital.</strong> If the court approves the application, it notifies the other spouse by SMS and gives them access to the documents. Both spouses then receive the case management session date by SMS, and both, or their legal representatives, must attend. The court says it aims to finalise the case within one month of registration.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The court&rsquo;s page lists Abu Dhabi residents and couples who married at that court as
        eligible, says the parties must come from a country not governed by sharia law, and warns that
        Arab League nationals may be asked for an official document proving their religion. Proceedings
        there run in English. Step-by-step detail is in
        <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/">how to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Federal courts: Sharjah, Ajman, Fujairah and Umm Al Quwain</h3>
      <p>
        Family cases in these four emirates go to the federal courts. The Ministry of Justice runs an
        e-Filing System for them and lists a family consultations service on its website. In November
        2025 the federal judiciary issued five regulations under the 2024 Personal Status Law, including
        Decision No. 67 of 2025 on Family Guidance, with the stated aim of expanding digital services.
        The ministry&rsquo;s English pages give little procedural detail, so confirm the current steps
        with the court where you file.
      </p>

      <h3>Ras Al Khaimah Courts</h3>
      <p>
        Ras Al Khaimah has its own local courts. Its family services sit on the Ras Al Khaimah government
        e-services portal, which the UAE Government portal links to for family cases. We could not load an
        official page describing the current online steps, so confirm them with RAK Courts directly.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box info-box--blue" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <h3>DIFC and ADGM are not an online shortcut</h3>
        <p>
          The DIFC Courts and ADGM Courts often come up in searches for digital courts, but they have no
          family jurisdiction and cannot grant a divorce. Their portals play no part in a UAE divorce.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Family Guidance -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="guidance-heading">
      <h2 id="guidance-heading">Family Guidance Online: Who Goes and Can You Attend by Video?</h2>
      <p>
        Family Guidance belongs to the 2024 Personal Status Law track used by Muslim couples, and even
        there it is not automatic. Under Article 8 of
        Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, the supervising judge may refer the spouses to Family Guidance
        before the case goes to court, if he or she thinks a settlement is feasible. There is no fixed
        guidance period in the law.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some cases skip it altogether: wills, inheritance, urgent and temporary cases, urgent or temporary
        orders on alimony, custody and tutorship, and cases where reconciliation is not foreseeable, such
        as cases to prove a marriage or to prove a divorce. If you settle, the agreement is written into a
        report signed by both spouses and the counsellor and approved by the supervising judge. It then
        has the force of an execution writ and cannot be appealed unless it contradicts the law.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whether the sessions themselves can be remote depends on the court:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Abu Dhabi:</strong> yes, by the ADJD&rsquo;s own description. Sessions are scheduled for videoconference over Webex, and staff contact you to change appointments.</li>
        <li><strong>Dubai:</strong> the file is opened online, and Dubai Courts&rsquo; personal status procedures guide, issued in January 2021, covers family counselling with the aid of telecommunication technologies. Whether a given session is by video is set by the counsellor, so ask when you are given a date.</li>
        <li><strong>Federal courts and RAK:</strong> we found no current official statement on video sessions. Ask the Family Guidance section when you register.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Non-Muslims skip this stage completely. Article 3 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 says
        divorce cases under that law are not referred to Family Guidance and go straight to court for a
        judgment at the first hearing. Either spouse can apply on the prescribed form without proving harm,
        and the divorce takes place by court decision once the other spouse has been duly served (Article
        8). In practice, that makes the civil track the one where &ldquo;online divorce&rdquo; is most
        real: one electronic filing, electronic service, one hearing, an electronic judgment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- From abroad -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="abroad-heading">
      <h2 id="abroad-heading">Filing for Divorce in the UAE From Abroad</h2>
      <p>
        You can usually start and run a UAE divorce from outside the country, provided a UAE court has
        jurisdiction and you appoint someone locally. Three things decide whether it works.
      </p>
      <h3>1. Which court can hear it</h3>
      <p>
        For Muslim couples, Article 3 of the 2024 law lets UAE courts hear cases against citizens and
        foreigners who have a domicile, residence or place of work in the State. A wife&rsquo;s divorce case
        can be filed where either spouse lives or works, or where the marital home is. If the respondent
        has no base in the UAE, the case goes to the plaintiff&rsquo;s court, otherwise to the court of
        the capital (Article 3(6)). Article 4 also lets a wife resident in the UAE sue a husband who had a
        base here but abandoned her and moved abroad, was deported, or whose address abroad is unknown.
      </p>
      <h3>2. An elected domicile and a representative</h3>
      <p>
        A plaintiff with no domicile in the UAE must name an elected domicile and a legal representative
        in the claim (Article 44(2)(a) of the Civil Procedure Code). Under Article 52, you can appear
        through a lawyer, or through a relative or in-law up to the fourth degree.
      </p>
      <h3>3. A power of attorney that the court will accept</h3>
      <p>
        A power of attorney signed abroad is not ready to use until it has been through the legalisation
        chain. Dubai Courts&rsquo; service pages spell it out: a document issued outside the UAE must be
        certified by the foreign ministry of the issuing country, then the UAE embassy there, then the UAE
        Ministry of Foreign Affairs, and translated into Arabic by a legal translator approved by the
        Ministry of Justice. The UAE is not a party to the Hague Apostille Convention, so an apostille on
        its own does not replace these steps.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once the case is running, the court can take remote steps outside the emirate&rsquo;s territory,
        coordinating with the authority where the person or documents are (Article 332), and can use remote
        technology for judicial assistance with foreign countries under ratified treaties (Article 337).
        That makes a video appearance from abroad possible, but it remains the judge&rsquo;s decision
        (Article 331), so ask before the hearing date rather than on the morning.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rules above are from the 2024 Muslim personal status law. The 2022 civil law has its own
        scope: Article 1 covers non-Muslim UAE nationals and non-Muslim foreigners residing in the State.
        If you are a non-Muslim couple and neither of you lives in the UAE any more, take advice on
        jurisdiction before you pay any fee.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Documents -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="documents-heading">
      <h2 id="documents-heading">Documents to Scan, and How Translation Works</h2>
      <p>
        For an online filing in Dubai, every document goes in as a PDF translated into Arabic, while the
        Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court works in English. Dubai Courts&rsquo; service pages require soft
        copies in PDF format, translated by a legal translator approved by the Ministry of Justice, with
        foreign documents legalised abroad and attested in the UAE first. Expect the same Arabic
        requirement at the federal courts and in Ras Al Khaimah.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Marriage certificate,</strong> legalised and attested if issued abroad, plus its Arabic translation. In Abu Dhabi&rsquo;s civil court, a copy in English and Arabic.</li>
        <li><strong>Passport and Emirates ID</strong> for both spouses, or at least yours and whatever identifying details you have for your spouse.</li>
        <li><strong>The statement of claim</strong> and a docket of supporting documents, for a Dubai personal status claim.</li>
        <li><strong>The Family Conciliation referral,</strong> where your case went through guidance.</li>
        <li><strong>Children&rsquo;s birth certificates,</strong> if custody, maintenance or travel are part of the case.</li>
        <li><strong>Power of attorney,</strong> fully legalised and translated, if someone files for you.</li>
        <li><strong>Any written settlement</strong> you want the court to approve.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Scan in colour, one PDF per document, with the translation attached behind the original. Name each
        file plainly (&ldquo;marriage-certificate-attested.pdf&rdquo;). A blurred attestation stamp can
        lead to a request to re-upload, which costs time. The complete checklist,
        including what each emirate asks for, is in
        <a href="/en/documents-for-divorce-uae/">documents required for divorce in the UAE</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Tracking -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="tracking-heading">
      <h2 id="tracking-heading">How to Track Your Case and Get the Judgment</h2>
      <p>
        You track a UAE divorce case through the court&rsquo;s own account system or app, using your case
        number. There is no national search across all courts.
      </p>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Dubai</h3>
          <p>
            Logged in, your files appear in your account. The public site also lists inquiry services for
            case details, &ldquo;smart&rdquo; verdicts, case applications and ishhadat certificates, which
            work from a case, application or certificate reference number.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Abu Dhabi</h3>
          <p>
            The AD Judicial app shows your file, its status in the courts, scheduled dates and
            notifications, and lets you submit requests on the file. The court also sends SMS notices to
            the number you gave when filing.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Federal courts and RAK</h3>
          <p>
            Use the Ministry of Justice e-Filing System or the Ras Al Khaimah e-services portal, depending
            on where you filed, and keep the case number from your first receipt.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        A note on searching by Emirates ID: we could not find an official Dubai Courts public search that
        works from an Emirates ID number alone. The Emirates ID checks you may read about online are
        usually Dubai Police services for financial criminal cases and travel bans, not family court
        files.
      </p>
      <h3>Getting the judgment and certificate</h3>
      <p>
        The output of a Dubai personal status claim is an electronic first-instance judgment, and the
        Civil Procedure Code lets certified copies be delivered electronically (Article 131(3)), with the
        writ of execution and an electronic signature on an enforceable copy obtained remotely (Article
        131(1)). Under Article 9 of the 2024 law, the appeal period in personal status matters is 30 days,
        counted from the day after the judgment if it was issued in your presence, otherwise from the day
        after you were notified (Article 10). Where a divorce was recorded as a declaration in Dubai
        rather than decided in a claim, the result is an ishhad document instead of a judgment.
      </p>
      <p>
        For use abroad, an electronic copy is rarely the end of it. Foreign registries and courts commonly
        want an attested copy with a translation. See
        <a href="/en/divorce-certificate-attestation-uae/">UAE divorce certificate attestation</a> before
        you book any appointment back home.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Table -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="table-heading">
      <h2 id="table-heading">Step by Step: What Is Online and What Is Not</h2>
      <p>
        Most of the court process is online; the steps that are not are the ones that prove who you are
        or record a declaration.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Step</th><th>Online?</th><th>Where</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Open a court account</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Yes</span></td>
              <td>UAE PASS, then the court portal</td>
              <td>No UAE PASS? A lawyer or a government service centre can file for you.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Family Guidance request (Muslim couples)</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Yes</span></td>
              <td>Dubai Courts Family Conciliation; ADJD e-services</td>
              <td>No fee in Dubai. Referral is the judge&rsquo;s call (Article 8, 2024 law).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Guidance sessions</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--rec">Partly</span></td>
              <td>ADJD by Webex; Dubai and others case by case</td>
              <td>Not used for non-Muslim civil divorce.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>File the divorce claim</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Yes</span></td>
              <td>Dubai Courts website or app; MOJ e-Filing; RAK portal</td>
              <td>Abu Dhabi civil divorce goes in through a typing centre.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Pay the court fee</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Yes</span></td>
              <td>Online payment after the fee notice</td>
              <td>Within 3 business days or the registration is void (Article 47(1)).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Serve your spouse</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Yes</span></td>
              <td>The court sends it</td>
              <td>SMS, email, apps; effective from sending (Articles 9 and 11).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Defence and documents</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Yes</span></td>
              <td>Court portal</td>
              <td>10 business days after service (Article 48(2)).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Hearings</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--rec">Partly</span></td>
              <td>Video, at the court&rsquo;s discretion</td>
              <td>Any party may ask for in person (Article 333).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Judgment</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Yes</span></td>
              <td>Electronic judgment, e-certified copies</td>
              <td>Article 131; e-signatures valid (Article 338).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Appeal</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Yes</span></td>
              <td>Court portal</td>
              <td>30 days in personal status matters (Article 9, 2024 law).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
