          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Based on the Civil Procedure Code and the courts&rsquo; service pages as published in 2025 and
          2026. Portals change; confirm on the court&rsquo;s own site when you file.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Warning -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="warning-heading">
      <h2 id="warning-heading">&ldquo;Online Divorce&rdquo; Websites: What They Cannot Do</h2>
      <p>
        No website, app or agent can divorce you in the UAE. A UAE divorce is created or recorded by a
        court: a judgment, a Family Guidance settlement approved by the supervising judge, or a talaq
        documented before the competent court. Anything else is, at best, a form-filling service.
      </p>
      <p>
        Typing centres and legal translators do real work, and the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court requires
        the first. The trouble starts when a site sells something the law does not offer. Be wary of:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>&ldquo;Divorce in 7 days&rdquo;.</strong> No one controls a court calendar. Even the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court describes one month from registration as an aim, not a promise.</li>
        <li><strong>&ldquo;No court needed&rdquo;.</strong> A certificate from a website, or a private agreement signed online, is not a UAE divorce.</li>
        <li><strong>&ldquo;DIFC or ADGM divorce online&rdquo;.</strong> Neither court can grant a divorce.</li>
        <li><strong>&ldquo;UAE apostille included&rdquo;.</strong> The UAE uses the attestation chain, not apostilles.</li>
        <li><strong>A foreign online divorce sold as a UAE solution.</strong> A divorce from another country&rsquo;s court is a foreign judgment, and whether it is recognised here is a separate legal question. A paper from a site that is not a court is not a divorce anywhere.</li>
        <li><strong>Requests for your UAE PASS login.</strong> Your UAE PASS works as your digital signature. Nobody legitimate needs your password; a lawyer files under a power of attorney.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>A talaq still has to be documented</h3>
      <p>
        For Muslim couples, a talaq pronounced by the husband does not end the paperwork. Article 58 of
        the 2024 Personal Status Law requires the husband to document the divorce before the competent
        court within 15 days of it happening, and this does not affect the wife&rsquo;s right to bring a
        case to prove the divorce. If he misses the deadline without an excuse the court accepts, the wife
        is entitled to compensation equal to alimony from the date of the divorce to the date it is
        documented. Documentation is a legal duty with a financial penalty, and in Dubai the court&rsquo;s
        documentation service is described as an in-person step (you or an agent with a special power of
        attorney), so do not plan on finishing it from a screen without checking the current channel first.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Nationality -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="nationality-heading">
      <h2 id="nationality-heading">Does Your Nationality Change the Online Process?</h2>
      <p>
        No. A Filipino, Indian, British or Emirati filer uses the same portal, uploads the same kind of
        PDFs and pays the same fees. Nationality affects which law applies and what happens to the
        judgment back home.
      </p>
      <p>
        For non-Muslim foreigners, Article 1 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 applies the civil law
        unless one spouse insists on the law of their home country. If that law limits divorce, the
        insistence changes the whole case, so settle the choice of law before you file. Recognition is the
        second question: a UAE judgment does not register itself abroad. See our guide to
        <a href="/en/filipino-divorce-uae/">Filipino divorce in the UAE</a> for what happens in the
        Philippines.
      </p>
